course: dictionary
lesson: 9753
title: Superstition: luck without proof
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A superstition is a belief that something brings good or bad luck, even though there is no real reason or proof for it. «Superstitions feel true because we remember the times they seemed to work and forget the times they did not.» So a belief can feel true without ever being checked.
explain: Look at the words around the idea. «It is only superstition» means the belief has nothing real behind it. A superstition is a rule people keep anyway, such as walking under a ladder or touching wood, even though there is no evidence that it changes anything.
explain: Superstitions can be fun, and many are old customs. «In medieval Europe some people linked black cats with witches, but in Britain and Japan black cats can mean good luck.» Two places, two different beliefs about the same cat: that is a sign a superstition is not a fact.
explain: A good thinker asks: is there real proof? If someone says the number 13 is unlucky, ask what evidence there is. Test the claim, look for proof, and compare it with what you can really check.
fact: A superstition is a belief that an object, action or number brings good or bad luck.
fact: There is no evidence that black cats, walking under ladders or the number 13 bring bad luck.
fact: There is no evidence that crossing your fingers brings good luck.
fact: «Knock on wood» and «touch wood» are old phrases linked to superstition.
fact: «Cats do not suck the breath out of babies; that is an old superstition.»
exercise: A superstition is a belief that brings luck, with no real reason behind it. What is a superstition? | A kind of sport | *A belief with no real reason or proof behind it | A rule from a science book | A proven fact about the weather
exercise: Which one is a superstition? | The Sun gives us light | *Black cats bring bad luck | Ice is cold | Plants need water
exercise: Why is the number 13 not a real cause of bad luck? | It is bigger than 12 | Everyone agrees it is unlucky | *There is no evidence that it brings bad luck | It is an odd number
test: «Superstitions feel true because we remember the times they seemed to work and forget the times they did not.» Why do superstitions feel true? | Because scientists have proved them | Because they are in every textbook | *Because we forget the times they did not work | Because they always come true
test: Which is an old phrase linked to superstition? | Plant the seed in spring | Add two cups of flour | Open the window wide | *Knock on wood
exam: What is the difference between a fact and a superstition? | They are the same thing, only written differently | *A fact can be checked with proof, but a superstition has no real reason behind it | A superstition is always true, and a fact is sometimes true | A fact is always old, but a superstition is always new
